  "readme": "# Game Dialogue Maker — GDevelop playground\n\nThe downloadable apple-quest demo: talk to Mira, close the conversation, touch\nthe red apple with green leaves, then return to Mira. Pickup removes the apple\nand sets numeric `hasApple = 1`; Mira's root conditions select the request at\n`hasApple = 0` and the thank-you at `hasApple = 1`. Returning before pickup still\nshows the request. Restart Play/Preview to reset. The pickup is enabled only\nwhen the source demo's `demoAppleQuest` flag is true.\n\n1. Extract the ZIP to a local folder, keeping assets/ beside game.json.\n2. In the current GDevelop 5 desktop editor, open game.json.\n3. Click Preview. Move with WASD or arrows and touch an NPC to talk.\n4. Click choices, scroll long text, and close with Escape or the close button.\n   Leave the NPC and return to restart its dialogue.\n\nThe scene contains a native Sprite player, an NPC Sprite and Text label for every\ncharacter, and a following camera for larger casts. Instances are visible and\neditable in GDevelop's scene editor. The simple white PNG is tinted at runtime.\nNative GDevelop sprite collision masks detect contact; NPCs are not obstacles.\nMovement pauses while dialogue or test variables are open.\n\nA JavaScript event in the scene drives movement and dialogue. Its accessible\nHTML overlay has scrollable text, choice buttons, and test-variable controls.\nIt supports newline pages, questions, answer branches, Next jumps, conditions,\nempty characters, and simulated fight outcomes. All conditions on a link must\npass; normal nodes follow the first allowed link. Next is used only if there are\nno outgoing links. Variables start at zero (numbers) or empty text (strings).\nBoth desktop preview and normal web exports have the DOM required by the UI.\nTouch/gamepad movement and multiplayer are not implemented.\n\nDialogue is stored as JSON in the GDMDialogue scene variable, and original editor\ndata is included in dialogue-source.json. Editing that standalone JSON alone does\nnot update the game. Re-export after editing in Dialogue Maker, or update the\nscene variable directly. User text is rendered as textContent, never HTML/code.\nNames are data, so duplicate names and punctuation do not affect object IDs.\n\nFor integration, runtimeScene.__gdmDialogue.variables contains mutable condition\nvalues; call runtimeScene.__gdmDialogue.render() while a conversation is active\nafter changing those values externally. Scene unload removes UI and listeners;\nscene pause/resume hides and restores the overlay. Keep the Player and NPC_N\nobject names when replacing their artwork. Add ordinary GDevelop events around\nthe included JavaScript event to extend the game.\n\nThe base project was generated by GDevelop's official libGD.js 5.6.283 core.\nNo extensions, external services, or extra code files need to be installed to\nuse this export. All game assets are local. The GDevelop editor itself may need\nits normal network access. Use desktop open for the ZIP's relative asset paths.\n\nValidation covers resource references, embedded event syntax, dialogue logic,\nand core-library project deserialization when LIBGD_JS is supplied. A browser\nharness tests movement/contact and DOM controls with simulated engine APIs.\nThese checks do not establish a successful graphical GDevelop Preview run.\n\nEngine smoke test: Preview, move to both NPCs, advance dialogue pages, choose\neach branch, try win/loss, set keys to 1 to unlock the gate, close, leave/re-enter,\nand test the empty character. Also verify long text, many choices, and scene\npause/resume/unload when integrating the scene into another game.\n\nReferences:\nhttps://docs.gdevelop.io/GDJS%20Runtime%20Documentation/classes/gdjs.RuntimeObject.html\nhttps://github.com/GDevelopApp/GDevelop-examples/tree/main/examples/javascript-blocks-in-platformer\n"
